Plan: Avoid a shared counting critical section by giving each worker exclusive ownership of one histogram.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <assert.h>
static const int input[8]={0,1,2,1,0,2,2,1};
static int partial[2][3],ids[2]={0,1};
static void *count_range(void *p) {
    int id=*(int *)p;
    for(int i=4*id;i<4*(id+1);i++) ++partial[id][input[i]];
    return NULL;
}
int main(void) {
    pthread_t t[2]; for(int i=0;i<2;i++) assert((pthread_create(&t[i],NULL,count_range,&ids[i])) == 0);
    for(int i=0;i<2;i++) assert((pthread_join(t[i],NULL)) == 0);
    int total[3]; for(int b=0;b<3;b++) total[b]=partial[0][b]+partial[1][b];
    assert(total[0]==2 && total[1]==3 && total[2]==3); return 0;
}
```

Why it is correct: The input is immutable and different workers write different array elements. Joins precede merging, providing the completion ordering. No shared read-modify-write needs a mutex; this partitioning design should not be confused with the formal progress class of a lock-free atomic algorithm.

